<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 16: The New Effects of Special Relativity</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> Reading covers sections 3.1 through 3.6. Do not skip the conversation between
      the thnking student and the bird on page 64.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability and upload your work to the 
      <q>Quiz 16</q> assignment in Canvas.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	In a given frame, a clock reads noon when a ball hits it. Do observers in all other frames agree that 
            the clock reads noon when the ball hits it?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	In pre-relativity physics, forces and accelerations are absolute, unlike the statement on page 59 of the 
            textbook. What changes in relativity that makes accelerations no longer absolute? 
            [Think about this for ~5 min and give your best answer]
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Using the postulates of special relativity, explain carefully and precisely why the observer on the 
            ground can say that the events mentioned on page 62 of the textbook are simultaneous.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Describe how the concept of simultaneity enters into the measurement of the length of a rod.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>
